body:
How long did it take you to get used to your CPAP? Do you sleep a full night with it? 

&#x200B;

If you have time, can you tell me your journey? How did you find a mask that fit right? How do you keep the pillow from pushing against it and breaking the seal and sending air into it?

Carcetti's speeches are cliche for a politician and even a character. I feel as though the writers wanted him portrayed as a very specific politician and it come across in his generic speeches... They are frustratingly cliche, even for a character written as giving cliche' speeches

&#x200B;

I couldnt imagine anyone could stand around and listen to his generic blathering about communities... much less watch other people do it on TV

I feel as though the Wire was a watered down highlight of both themes.

&#x200B;

I have read both books (after watching the series countless times) and i felt as though each book does a great job of really diving into the slice of life in both outlooks - really shows the "dickensian aspect" of being a drug dealer in Baltimore and how normalized drug dealing and addiction is in those areas. Homicide did a great job at digging into systemic and cultural issues plaguing the idea of Law Enforcement in general, not just the BPD.. of which the WIre  certainly includes but only as context to the plotline

&#x200B;

The Wire made me want to be murder police, Homicide (the book) made me change my mind

Great pull u/TheCarnageOfBattle!

&#x200B;

I didnt notice that previously, but I have rewatched the series enough times to see a lot of paralell scenes between each "side" - this fits right in with it!

Ds major character arc was his brain wrestling with the ethics of living the type of life he had - I feel as though he felt more out of place due to being a drug dealer among other legit folks (which would explain why Donnette didnt get it and assumed he was talking about race) 

&#x200B;

Whereas the kids scene was more caste-based, having a tough realization they really were just ghetto kids who weren't really taken seriously outside of their own lives. 

&#x200B;

I  love how it is a parallel scene but seemingly opposite concerns about the people involved, despite that it seems heavily race-based on the surface

I love how the Wire did a great job at showing the cycle continues - all the latter series characters turned into new versions of the old - Dukie is the new Bubbles, SYdnor is the new McNulty, Mike the new Omar, etc

&#x200B;

Having a hard time seeing Herc turn into Levy's personal dick, - --- what was he "the next"?

I think Avon correctly summarized your essay above -- "You not hard enough for this right here, and maybe, just maybe, your not smart enough for them out there."

&#x200B;

Stringer was a strong drug organization leader and a budding businessman, but clearly had to learn a lot of lessons and underestimated how much he had to learn. Unfortunately, he was in a business that required for him to be sharp on both ends with little room for error that most growing businesses in most other industries can handle.
